---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/canada-tariffs/">Canada tariffs</a><span>September 8, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Ottawa and Washington &middot; July 20 to September 8, 2026</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />

      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#outlets">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#claim-check">Claim checked against the record</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-happened-next">What happened next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
      </details></nav>
      <div class="kpi-strip" data-layer="fact">
        {event.kpis.map((k) => (
          <div class="kpi">
            <span class="kpi-value">{k.value}<small>{k.unit ? ` ${k.unit}` : ""}</small></span>
            <span class="kpi-label">{k.label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <aside class="three-things" data-layer="fact">
        <p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Since August 22, the United States has charged an extra 50 percent on three groups of Canadian goods: alcoholic beverages, dairy, and motor vehicles. Since September 8, Canada charges 15, 25, or 50 percent extra on {listItems} tariff lines of American goods, which Finance Canada says cover C${financeCoveredCad} billion of imports.<Cite s="wh-fact-sheet-2026-07-20" passage="three Proclamations pursuant to Section 338 of the Tariff Act of 1930" /><Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="products covering $27.6 billion in imports from the U.S." /><Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0786" passage="This Order comes into force on the day on which the United States Surtax Order (2026) comes into force" /></li>
          <li>Canada's list is two cabinet orders put together: {oic0785Items} lines in a new order at the three rates, and {oic0786Items} steel and aluminum lines added to last year's order at 25 and 50 percent. Between the August 25 and August 26 versions of the list, every fish and seafood line came off and nine industrial lines went on, and the dollar figure did not change.<Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="List updated as of August 26, 2026" /><Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0785" passage="are subject to a surtax" /></li>
          <li>The same Tuesday Canada's charge began, the United States went further than a duty. From September 29, listed Canadian dairy, motorcycle, and alcoholic-beverage products may not enter the country at all, and the White House said the President had ordered US${gsaDirectedUsd} billion of Canadian-made products taken off the catalogues federal agencies buy from.<Cite s="proc-exclude-dairy-2026-09-08" passage="12:01 a.m. eastern time on September 29, 2026" /><Cite s="wh-fact-sheet-2026-09-08" passage="remove $50 billion dollars" /></li>
        </ol>
      </aside>
      <div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context">
        <p class="sources-line">This story rests on the White House fact sheets of July 20 and September 8, the three Section 338 proclamations and their July 20 annex PDFs, Proclamation 11056 as published in the Federal Register, the motor-vehicles proclamation's public-inspection PDF, CBP cargo-systems message 69606660 of August 21, 19 U.S.C. 1338, Prime Minister Carney's August 22 remarks, the Department of Finance Canada release of August 25 and the product tables of August 25 and August 26, the August 27 Sarnia Finance release, the Spring Economic Update 2026 intro, Gazette SOR/2025-181, the Bank of Canada USD/CAD rate for August 25, staff analytical note 2025-18, and the September 2 rate decision, the Employment and Social Development Canada release of August 28, Orders in Council PC 2026-0785 and PC 2026-0786, Canada Border Services Agency Customs Notices 26-23 and 25-11, the motor-vehicle remission order in the Canada Gazette, the Census Bureau's goods-with-Canada series, Statistics Canada's Daily for December 2025, the USTR Canada page and USTR's July 20, February 20, and November 2023 statements, the 2021 USMCA dairy panel report, CRS report R48435, the Supreme Court's February 20, 2026 opinion in No. 24-1287, the White House August 25 reply, the September 8 White House proclamations including the alcohol-basket scope change and their annex PDFs, the White House presidential-actions listings of September 8 and September 9, the Federal Register public-inspection page of September 9, Carney's September 8 words as carried by the Winnipeg Free Press from the YouTube transcript and the YouTube watch page for that video, the June 15 Prime Minister readout on the Marconi SAFE contract, the Major Projects Office projects page, the Quebec government release of September 7, Global Affairs Canada's CUSMA joint-review page and May 2026 monthly trade report, and coverage checked against those records. Days, item counts, and dollar conversions are counted from those files.</p>
      </div>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Seventeen days from the U.S. duties to Canada's</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 8, Canada began charging 15, 25, or 50 percent extra on C${financeCoveredCad} billion of goods arriving from the United States, its answer to the 50 percent the United States had started charging on the same value of Canadian goods on August 22. A tariff is a tax collected at the border on an imported good, set as a share of the good's value. Finance Canada's release of August 25 set the terms: match the U.S. tariffs "dollar for dollar, rate for rate," and C${supportPackage} billion to help the workers and businesses hit by both sides' charges.<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="dollar for dollar, rate for rate" /></p>

        <QuoteCard speaker="François-Philippe Champagne, minister of finance" when="August 25, 2026" where="Finance Canada news release">
          <p>When the United States asked too much and offered too little, we chose to stand up for Canadians.<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="When the United States asked too much and offered too little, we chose to stand up for Canadians" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The American duties came from three proclamations Trump signed on July 20 under Section 338 of the Tariff Act of 1930, a law that lets a president add up to 50 percent when he finds another country discriminating against U.S. trade. One was built around alcohol, one around dairy, one around motor vehicles. The White House said the provinces had pulled U.S. alcohol from their store shelves, cutting those imports by about 81 percent, or $582 million, in a year, and that Canada's cheese import quotas treat Europe better than the United States. The duties were set for August 19; an August 18 proclamation moved them to August 22, and they took effect then.<Cite s="wh-fact-sheet-2026-07-20" passage="three Proclamations pursuant to Section 338 of the Tariff Act of 1930" /><Cite s="proc-exclude-dairy-2026-09-08" passage="12:01 a.m. eastern time on September 29, 2026" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Carney broke off the trade talks the evening of August 21 and announced the matching tariffs the next morning, saying Canada took the step "reluctantly" because "it will raise costs and reduce choice for Canadians." He said Canada had been ready to drop its remaining tariffs on steel, aluminum, and autos if Washington cut its own substantially, to press the provinces to put U.S. alcohol back on shelves, and to tighten how it runs its dairy, poultry, and egg quota system without changing the system. Sovereignty, French language and culture, and what he called key industries "were never on the table".<Cite s="carney-remarks-2026-08-22" passage="match Washington’s new tariffs dollar for dollar" /> The White House answered on August 25 that it had offered Canada "the most preferential market access of any country on Earth" and that Canada had answered with "unreasonable demands, walk-backs, and flat-out rejection."<Cite s="wh-ending-free-ride-2026-08-25" passage="the most preferential market access of any country on Earth" /></p>

        <QuoteCard speaker="Mark Carney, prime minister" when="August 22, 2026" where="Remarks on the Canada-U.S. trade negotiations" label="Prime Minister's Office transcript">
          <p>Canada will match Washington’s new tariffs dollar for dollar in order to protect Canadian workers, farmers, families, and businesses.<Cite s="carney-remarks-2026-08-22" passage="match Washington’s new tariffs dollar for dollar" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Finance published its product list on August 25 with {listItemsAug25} tariff lines, a tariff line being one numbered entry in the customs list that classifies goods at the border. The version dated August 26 has {listItems}. Between the two versions the whole fish and seafood category, {removedCodes} lines, was removed, and nine industrial products were added at 50 percent, among them wood charcoal, gypsum board, glass containers, and copper wire. The C${financeCoveredCad} billion figure did not move, and Finance has not said why the list changed. Two days later a federal employment department release still listed fish and seafood among the goods facing the charge.<Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="List updated as of August 26, 2026" /><Cite s="esdc-countermeasures-2026-08-28" passage="fish and seafood" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The charge became law on September 4 through two cabinet orders, signed by the Governor General on the finance minister's advice. One creates the 2026 surtax order, surtax being the Canadian legal word for the extra charge, with {oic0785Items} items across the three rates; the other adds {oic0786Items} steel and aluminum codes to the 2025 order. That is {oicUnionItems} codes, the same {listItems} as Finance's August 26 table. Goods already on their way to Canada on September 8 are exempt.<Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0785" passage="comes into force on September 8, 2026" /><Cite s="cbsa-cn26-23" passage="in transit to Canada" /> The same Tuesday, Trump signed proclamations that go past duties to bans: from September 29, certain Canadian products, dairy and alcoholic beverages among them, may not enter the United States at all, which Section 338 allows once a president finds the discrimination continued. Seventeen days after the U.S. duties began, both countries were collecting, and the next American step is exclusion, not a higher rate.<Cite s="proc-exclude-alcohol-2026-09-08" passage="additional 50 percent levy on U.S. alcoholic beverages" /></p>

        <QuoteCard speaker="Mark Carney, prime minister" when="September 8, 2026" where="Video address, as transcribed by the Winnipeg Free Press" label="Winnipeg Free Press transcript of the YouTube address">
          <p>I don’t believe in escalating the conflict, that’s not constructive, but our tariffs are necessary to protect our workers, companies and communities.<Cite s="wfp-carney-forward-guidance-2026-09-08" passage="I don’t believe in escalating the conflict, that’s not constructive" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>From the U.S. proclamations to Canada's order</strong><span>Dates from the White House proclamations and annexes, Carney's remarks, Finance Canada, the Order in Council, and CBSA.</span></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={timeline} />
          <p class="chart-source">Seventeen days separate the U.S. duties taking effect on August 22 and Canada's order taking effect on September 8.<Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0785" passage="comes into force on September 8, 2026" /></p>
        </figure>

        <SourcedBlock source="cbp-csms-69606660-2026-08-21" kind="record" detail>
          <p>U.S. Customs and Border Protection's filing notice of August 21 tells importers which line of the tariff schedule to use for the new charge. It also created lines for goods the proclamations leave out: those goods carry no Section 338 charge, though other existing duties on them still apply.<Cite s="cbp-csms-69606660-2026-08-21" passage="shall continue to be subject to antidumping, countervailing, or other duties" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="oic-pc-2026-0785" kind="record" detail>
          <p>PC 2026-0785: goods originating in the United States classified under Schedule 1, 2, or 3 "are subject to a surtax" of 15, 25, or 50 percent of the value for duty. Goods "in transit to Canada on the day on which this Order comes into force" are excepted.<Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0785" passage="in transit to Canada on the day on which this Order comes into force" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="cbsa-cn26-23" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Customs Notice 26-23, Ottawa, September 7, 2026: effective September 8, listed U.S.-origin goods "are subject to a surtax of either 15%, 25% or 50% of the value for duty." Origin is the CUSMA marking rule. Remission is a write-off of a surtax "paid or payable," so it can stop a payment or refund one. The 2025 remission order and the U.S. Remission Framework remain available.<Cite s="cbsa-cn26-23" passage="surtax of either 15%, 25% or 50% of the value for duty" /><Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0785" passage="remission is granted of surtaxes paid or payable" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>The count fell. The dollar did not.</h3>
              The public list shrank by {listCountDrop} lines when every fish and seafood tariff item came off and nine industrial lines went on. Finance's C${financeCoveredCad} billion did not move. Why it matters: Finance still puts the same dollar figure on the list after the whole seafood category left it, and it has not published how the figure was calculated.<Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="List updated as of August 26, 2026" /><Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="products covering $27.6 billion in imports from the U.S." /> <a class="catch-jump" href="#claim-check">Claim checked</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>A duty, then a ban, the same Tuesday.</h3>
              Finance says Canada answered a 50 percent U.S. duty on listed goods. The same day, the United States signed proclamations that turn part of its own 50 percent duty into an import ban from September 29: {annexDairyBanItems} dairy-basket lines including whey, {annexMotorBanItems} motorcycle line, and {annexAlcoholBanItems} alcoholic-beverage lines. It also changed the alcoholic-beverages basket from September 15: {annexAlcoholScopeAddItems} HTSUS lines added at 50 percent, {annexAlcoholScopeRemoveItems} taken off. Why it matters: a Canadian exporter of the banned lines does not pay a higher U.S. duty. The goods are barred. The added alcohol-basket lines stay dutiable, at 50 percent, from September 15.<Cite s="annex-i-alcohol-scope-2026-09-08" passage="New Products Covered by Section 338 Tariffs" /> <a class="catch-jump" href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Not every good in the U.S. baskets pays the 50 percent.</h3>
              U.S. customs gave the goods the proclamations leave out their own filing lines, with no Section 338 charge; other duties on them still apply. Why it matters: Finance's rate-for-rate claim is not a claim against every good in those three baskets.<Cite s="cbp-csms-69606660-2026-08-21" passage="9903.03.16: Articles of civil aircraft" /> <a class="catch-jump" href="#what-happened">What happened</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>The fight reaches federal catalogues.</h3>
              The White House fact sheet of September 8 says the President directed the U.S. Trade Representative and the General Services Administration to remove US${gsaDirectedUsd} billion of Canadian-origin products from Multiple Award Schedules, the catalogues federal agencies buy from. The fact sheet does not report a removal. Why it matters: the confrontation is no longer only a border duty. It now names government purchasing, at a claimed scale larger than Finance's C${financeCoveredCad} billion list.<Cite s="wh-fact-sheet-2026-09-08" passage="remove $50 billion dollars" /> <a class="catch-jump" href="#what-happened-next">What happened next</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>The Canadian charge is at Canada's border.</h3>
              Carney said the step "will raise costs and reduce choice for Canadians."<Cite s="carney-remarks-2026-08-22" passage="raise costs and reduce choice for Canadians" /> CBSA makes the importer declare the surtax. The Bank of Canada's 2018 staff note found "high but incomplete pass-through of tariffs to consumer goods prices." Its April 2025 scenarios "assume that 75% of the increased costs from tariffs is passed on to consumer prices within six quarters." Those are a finding and an assumption, not the same thing. The September 2 decision said the measures "could feed into consumer prices over time." Why it matters: the charge is due from the importer unless an exception or remission stops it. Whether a household pays more is not measured here.<Cite s="cbsa-cn26-23" passage="Importers must declare imported goods as subject to a surtax" /><Cite s="boc-san-2025-18" passage="high but incomplete pass-through of tariffs to consumer goods prices" /><Cite s="boc-fad-2026-09-02" passage="could feed into consumer prices over time" /> <a class="catch-jump" href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Two annual trade totals, and a three-rate list</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The two countries traded about US$715 billion in goods in 2025. The Census Bureau counts US${computed.censusExports2025Rounded} billion of American goods going north and US${computed.censusImports2025Rounded} billion of Canadian goods coming south.<Cite s="census-c1220-canada" passage="TOTAL 2025" /> Statistics Canada, counting its own way, says Canada sold 5.8 percent less to the United States in 2025 than the year before and bought 2.9 percent less, and that the United States still took 71.7 percent of everything Canada exported, down from 75.9 percent in 2024.<Cite s="statcan-daily-2025-12" passage="merchandise trade surplus with the United States totalled $81.6 billion" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Against those totals, the goods in this fight are a slice. Finance Canada's C${financeCoveredCad} billion is about US${computed.usdCoveredRounded} billion at the Bank of Canada's August 25 rate, and the U.S. Trade Representative put the American duties at "nearly $20 billion in imports from Canada".<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="products covering $27.6 billion in imports from the U.S." /><Cite s="ustr-section-338-2026-07-20" passage="nearly $20 billion in imports from Canada" /> The White House fact sheet of July 20 names the rate, the law, and the three baskets, but no dollar figure.<Cite s="wh-fact-sheet-2026-07-20" passage="three Proclamations pursuant to Section 338 of the Tariff Act of 1930" /> What is not a slice is the rate. The listed lines pay 15, 25, or 50 percent of their value, in a trading relationship Canada's own spring update had described, before any of this, as "approximately 85% of our goods trade tariff-free," with an average Canadian tariff of 5.2 percent against a world average of 11.4.<Cite s="budget-seu-2026-intro" passage="With approximately 85% of our goods trade tariff-free" /><Cite s="budget-seu-2026-intro" passage="Average Tariff Rate" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Section 338 had never been used. The Congressional Research Service wrote in February 2025 that the United States had never imposed tariffs under it, and no court has ruled on a Section 338 duty.<Cite s="crs-r48435" passage="As of the time of this writing, the United States has never imposed tariffs under" /> The July 20 proclamations came five months after the Supreme Court held, in Learning Resources v. Trump, that the International Emergency Economic Powers Act, the 1977 law the administration had used for its earlier tariffs, "does not authorize the President to impose tariffs."<Cite s="scotus-24-1287" passage="Held: IEEPA does not authorize the President to impose tariffs." /> The trade representative said that day that "alternative tools would be implemented" and named three of them; Section 338 was not on the list.<Cite s="ustr-ieepa-2026-02-20" passage="alternative tools would be implemented" /> Besides the duty of up to 50 percent, the law lets the President declare that a country's products "shall be excluded from importation", and the September 8 proclamations use that power.<Cite s="usc-19-1338" passage="shall be excluded from importation" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The law also sets a clock: new duties are levied "thirty days after the date of such proclamation".<Cite s="usc-19-1338" passage="thirty days after the date of such proclamation there shall be levied, collected, and paid upon the articles enumerated in such proclamation" /> The July 20 proclamations set August 19, {daysJulyProcToOriginalEffective} days later.<Cite s="proc-alcohol-2026-07-20" passage="12:01 a.m. eastern time on August 19, 2026" /> The September 8 changes make the newly added lines dutiable from September 15, {daysSeptModifyToEffective} days after, on the strength of the President's power to amend an earlier proclamation.<Cite s="proc-modify-motor-2026-09-08" passage="12:01 a.m. eastern time on September 15, 2026" /></p>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Finance Canada's August 26 table, by rate</strong><span>Tariff items counted from the product list Finance Canada published.</span></figcaption>
          <BarChart bars={rateBars} ariaLabel="Tariff items on the Finance Canada list by rate" />
          <p class="chart-source">{listRate50} lines at 50 percent, {listRate25} at 25 percent, {listRate15} at 15 percent, {listItems} in all.<Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="List updated as of August 26, 2026" /></p>
          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">Counted from the Finance Canada table dated August 26, 2026. The August 25 version has {listItemsAug25} lines.</p>
        </figure>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The Finance list is the two orders, line for line</strong><span>Canadian tariff items counted from the English schedules of PC 2026-0785 and PC 2026-0786 and from the Finance August 26 HTML table. U.S. July 20 Annex I counted as unique HTSUS 8-digit lines, a different unit.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Count", "What it is", "How it was counted"]} rows={orderCountRows} />
          <p class="chart-source">{oic0785Items} plus {oic0786Items} is {oicUnionItems}, the same unique-code total as the Finance table, with {oicOverlap} overlap and {financeRateMismatches} rate mismatches.<Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0785" passage="Goods Subject to 15% Surtax" /><Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0786" passage="Goods Subject to 25% Surtax" /></p>
          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">English schedules only: PC 2026-0785 file offsets 7090 to 11003 for Schedules 1 to 3; PC 2026-0786 file offsets 3038 to 9151 for Schedules 1, 1.1, 2, and 2.1. Finance rates taken from the last column of each HTML table row.</p>
        </figure>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The coverage dollars against the Census 2025 export total</strong><span>Finance Canada's C${financeCoveredCad} billion converts to about US${computed.usdCoveredRounded} billion at the August 25 Bank of Canada rate. Census totals are in U.S. dollars.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Figure", "What it is", "Record"]} rows={moneyRows} />
          <p class="chart-source">Twenty billion is {computed.share20OfUsExportsPct} percent of the Census 2025 export total.<Cite s="census-c1220-canada" passage="333,619.7" /></p>
          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">Census annual totals taken from the Census Bureau goods-with-Canada table. Finance figures from the August 25 release. Rate from Bank of Canada valet FXUSDCAD for August 25, 2026.</p>
        </figure>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Finance's public list and the two cabinet orders are the same lines.<Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0786" passage="Schedules 1 and 2 to the Order are replaced by the Schedules 1 to 2.1 set out in the schedule to this Order" /> The U.S. July 20 annexes, counted the same way, run to {annexJulyItems} lines: {annexJulyDairyItems} dairy, {annexJulyAlcoholItems} alcohol, {annexJulyMotorItems} in the motor-vehicles basket. An American tariff line and a Canadian tariff item are different units, so the two counts do not compare.<Cite s="annex-i-dairy-2026-07-20" passage="A 50% ad valorem Section 338 tariff shall apply" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">This is a partial return to broad counter-tariffs. A year earlier, on September 1, 2025, Canada had removed its surtaxes on ${rollbackConsumer2025} billion of consumer goods and ${rollbackOther2025} billion of other goods, citing supply chains and consumer prices.<Cite s="gazette-sor-2025-181" passage="this Order comes into force on September 1, 2025" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The charge falls on goods that count as American under the origin rules of CUSMA, the Canada-United States-Mexico Agreement that replaced NAFTA in 2020.<Cite s="cbsa-cn26-23" passage="Determination of Country of Origin for the Purpose of Marking Goods (CUSMA Countries) Regulations" /> That agreement's first mandatory review fell on July 1, 2026. Canada and Mexico said they wanted it renewed for another 16 years; the United States did not agree, so the three countries now review it every year and it stays in force until 2036.<Cite s="cusma-joint-review" passage="The first Joint Review must take place on the 6th anniversary of the Agreement’s entry into force, which is July 1, 2026" /><Cite s="gac-monthly-2026-05" passage="The United States did not agree to renew the Agreement at this time" /> The records do not say that outcome led to the July 20 proclamations. It sits before them on the timeline.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="who-feels-it">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Who pays, and who loses a market</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The Canadian charge is paid at Canada's border by the importer, the business bringing the U.S. goods in, which must declare the surtax and pay it before the goods clear. It applies to commercial and personal shipments alike, small parcels included. What is on the list is what a shopper would recognize: milk powders and cheese, perfume, cotton T-shirts, smartphones, window air conditioners, furniture, and the steel and aluminum that had already been paying 25 percent and now pay 50.<Cite s="cbsa-cn26-23" passage="in transit to Canada" /><Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="List updated as of August 26, 2026" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Whether that reaches a household's bill is not measured yet. The Bank of Canada, holding its rate at 2.25 percent on September 2, wrote that the new U.S. tariffs and Canada's answer "will also raise costs for some businesses and could feed into consumer prices over time." Its own past work found that tariffs mostly pass through to consumer prices, and its planning scenarios assume three quarters of the cost does within a year and a half. Carney said it plainly on August 22: the step "will raise costs and reduce choice for Canadians", and some U.S. companies and states "are innocent bystanders in a dispute they did not want."<Cite s="boc-fad-2026-09-02" passage="could feed into consumer prices over time" /><Cite s="boc-san-2025-18" passage="high but incomplete pass-through of tariffs to consumer goods prices" /><Cite s="carney-remarks-2026-08-22" passage="match Washington’s new tariffs dollar for dollar" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Some importers pay nothing. The order does not charge goods already in transit, goods brought in to be re-exported, or imports for Campobello Island, and it refunds the surtax for public health, public safety, and national security uses and for goods used in manufacturing, food processing, and farming. Existing relief for automakers that keep producing in Canada stays in place.<Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0785" passage="comes into force on September 8, 2026" /><Cite s="gazette-si-2026-13-motor-remission" passage="United States Surtax Remission Order (Motor Vehicles 2026)" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On the Canadian side of the U.S. duties, the C${supportPackage} billion is aimed at the sectors Carney named as hit hardest, autos, forestry, steel, and aluminum, and comes "on top of nearly $25 billion already delivered". Ontario widened its Protect Ontario Financing Program, up to C$1 billion in loans for payroll, rent, and utilities, to any business whose exports face the new 50 percent, with Premier Doug Ford saying the province "will do whatever it takes to stand up for Ontario workers". Quebec reserved some public contracts for firms with a Quebec or Canadian establishment.<Cite s="wfp-carney-forward-guidance-2026-09-08" passage="I don’t believe in escalating the conflict, that’s not constructive" /><Cite s="ontario-ford-2026-08-24" passage="Ontario Newsroom" /><Cite s="quebec-frechette-2026-09-07" passage="Le Canada répliquera avec des contre-tarifs le 8 septembre" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The U.S. bans of September 29 fall on a narrower group of Canadian exporters, who do not pay a higher duty but lose the U.S. market for those lines outright: whey and molasses producers, makers of motorcycles over 800 cc, and brewers, wineries, and cider makers selling packaged drinks. From September 15 the U.S. 50 percent list also widens to golf carts, outboard motorboats, seats and furniture, specified cheeses, hides, and furskins, while salt, cement, and refined lead come off. The costs are uneven, and Carney said the U.S. tariffs "will hurt some Canadians more than others, and that was by design."<Cite s="annex-i-dairy-2026-09-08" passage="Whey protein concentrates" /><Cite s="annex-i-motor-vehicles-2026-09-08" passage="Motorcycles (including mopeds)" /><Cite s="annex-i-alcohol-2026-09-08" passage="Beer made from malt" /><Cite s="annex-i-auto-scope-2026-09-08" passage="New Products Covered by Section 338 Tariffs" /><Cite s="wfp-carney-forward-guidance-2026-09-08" passage="I don’t believe in escalating the conflict, that’s not constructive" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="outlets">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Errors first, then what checked out</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</h2>
        <p data-layer="fact">Thirteen outlets were checked against the primary records. Nine independent reporting lineages plus one transcript carrier, and fifteen cards below, including a coverage-style row for the September 8 White House fact sheet. PBS carried the Associated Press Toronto dispatch and is not a second lineage. MarketScreener, the Detroit News, and Investing.com carried Reuters. The Winnipeg Free Press carried a transcript of Carney's September 8 video. Errors first.</p>

        <div data-layer="fact">
        <OutletCheck outlet="Winnipeg Free Press" claim={`transcribed Carney saying new ports, mines, and energy corridors &ldquo;$500 billion in new private investment.&rdquo;`} verdict={`The Major Projects Office lists $192 billion in new investment and $500 billion in future private sector investment. The video uses new for the office's future figure.`} chip="mislabeled" status="warn"><Cite s="wfp-carney-forward-guidance-2026-09-08" passage="$500 billion in new private investment" /><Cite s="mpo-projects-transformative-strategies" passage="$192 billion in new investment" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="Winnipeg Free Press" claim={`transcribed Carney saying the Marconi SAFE contract was announced &ldquo;In July of this year.&rdquo;`} verdict={`The Prime Minister's readout of that announcement is dated June 15, 2026. The video's McKinley tariff history is the video's characterization, unchecked here.`} chip="mislabeled" status="warn"><Cite s="wfp-carney-forward-guidance-2026-09-08" passage="In July of this year" /><Cite s="pm-readout-marconi-2026-06-15" passage="Marconi Technologies has been awarded a contract exceeding $10 million" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="MarketScreener (Reuters)" claim={`wrote that the counter-tariffs impose duties &ldquo;across around 700 products imported from south of the border, a government statement said.&rdquo;`} verdict={`No government statement, table, or CBSA notice in this story's records prints 700. The August 25 Finance table has ${listItemsAug25} tariff items. Unconfirmed.`} chip="unconfirmed" status="warn"><Cite s="marketscreener-reuters-2026-08-25" passage="across around 700 products imported from south of the border, a government statement said" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="Associated Press" claim={`wrote that Section 338 tariff authority &ldquo;has never been used, let alone tested in court,&rdquo; and characterized the administration as having &ldquo;made no attempt to calculate the dollar amount of damage.&rdquo;`} verdict={`The never-used half checks out against CRS, which wrote in February 2025 that the United States had never imposed tariffs under Section 338. No court has ruled on a Section 338 duty in any record this story cites. The no-attempt-to-calculate line is AP's characterization, not a finding in the proclamations.`} chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="ap-section-338-2026-08-29" passage="has never been used, let alone tested in court" /><Cite s="crs-r48435" passage="As of the time of this writing, the United States has never imposed tariffs under" /><Cite s="ap-section-338-2026-08-29" passage="made no attempt to calculate the dollar amount of damage" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="USA Today" claim={`wrote that the U.S. imposed 50 percent tariffs on &ldquo;an estimated 5% worth of Canadian imports,&rdquo; and that Canada's reply covers &ldquo;about $20 billion worth of U.S. products.&rdquo;`} verdict={`USTR on July 20 valued the three Section 338 actions at nearly US$20 billion of imports from Canada. That figure is ${computed.share20OfUsImportsPct} percent of the Census 2025 U.S. import total from Canada (US$${computed.censusImports2025Rounded} billion). Estimated 5 percent is consistent with that arithmetic.`} chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="usatoday-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="estimated 5% worth of Canadian imports" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="The Hill" claim={`wrote that the tariffs cover &ldquo;more than 700 U.S. products&rdquo; and &ldquo;around 6 percent of what the U.S. exported to Canada last year,&rdquo; next to a &ldquo;$27.6 billion&rdquo; lead.`} verdict={`Six percent is US$20 billion against the Census 2025 export total of US$${computed.censusExports2025Rounded} billion: that arithmetic checks out. The 700-product count is unconfirmed. The Finance tables have ${listItemsAug25} tariff items on August 25 and ${listItems} on August 26, not 700 products.`} chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="hill-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="around 6 percent of what the U.S. exported to Canada last year" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="Al Jazeera" claim={`wrote that Canada &ldquo;would launch a $5.42bn support package&rdquo; and that the tariffs apply to &ldquo;nearly $20bn worth of US imports&rdquo; and &ldquo;more than 700 products.&rdquo;`} verdict={`US$${computed.usdSupport} billion is C$${supportPackage} billion divided by the Bank of Canada August 25 rate of ${fxUsdCad20260825}: consistent. The 700-product count is unconfirmed.`} chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="aljazeera-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="$5.42bn support package" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="The Detroit News (Reuters)" claim={`carried the Reuters dispatch converting Finance as &ldquo;C$27.6 billion ($19.94 billion)&rdquo; of U.S. goods, and the same 700-product line as MarketScreener.`} verdict={`C$${financeCoveredCad} billion at the Bank of Canada August 25 rate is US$${computed.usdCovered} billion: consistent. The text is Reuters, as MarketScreener carried it. The 700-product attribution is the same unconfirmed count.`} chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="detroitnews-canada-tariffs-2026-08-25" passage="C$27.6 billion ($19.94 billion)" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="BBC" claim={`wrote that the Canadian tariffs apply to &ldquo;nearly C$28bn ($20bn; £15bn)&rdquo; and that support programmes total &ldquo;C$7.5bn.&rdquo;`} verdict={`Finance Canada prints ${financeCoveredCad} billion and ${supportPackage} billion with no currency tag. The C$ conversion and the US$20 billion equivalent match the Bank of Canada rate as rounded.`} chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="bbc-canada-tariffs-2026-08-25" passage="nearly C$28bn ($20bn; £15bn)" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="The Guardian" claim={`wrote that the tariffs apply to products covering &ldquo;C$27.6bn in imports from the US&rdquo; and quoted the government that they do not apply to goods in transit.`} verdict="The C$ label matches how Finance Canada writes. The in-transit exception is in the Order and in Customs Notice 26-23." chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="guardian-canada-tariffs-2026-09-07" passage="C$27.6bn in imports from the US" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="CNBC" claim={`wrote that the duties range from 15% to 50% across hundreds of U.S. products &ldquo;worth a total $27.6 billion.&rdquo;`} verdict={`Finance Canada prints ${financeCoveredCad} billion. CNBC does not tag the currency. The figure is the same unlabeled total.`} chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="cnbc-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="worth a total $27.6 billion" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="CBC News" claim={`wrote that Finance Canada dropped seafood and fish products from the list and that the decision was based on &ldquo;feedback.&rdquo;`} verdict={`The August 26 table has none of the ${removedCodes} chapter 03 lines that were on the August 25 table, and it adds the nine industrial lines CBC named. The feedback reason is what CBC reported Finance said. It is not a line on either table.`} chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="cbc-seafood-tariffs-2026-08-27" passage={`the decision was based on "feedback."`} /><Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="4402.90.10 | Wood charcoal" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="Reuters, as carried by Investing.com" claim={`wrote that Canada removed seafood and fish products from the counter-tariff list, quoting Finance: &ldquo;including removing seafood and fish products from our list of counter tariffs.&rdquo;`} verdict={`The tables match the removal. This card reads the Reuters text as Investing.com carried it, because reuters.com could not be opened. The reason is what Reuters reported Finance posted, not a government record this story cites.`} chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="investing-reuters-seafood-2026-08-27" passage="including removing seafood and fish products from our list of counter tariffs" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="White House fact sheet, September 8" claim={`wrote that Canada imposed new retaliatory tariffs on &ldquo;about $20 billion of U.S. exports, including steel, dairy, and agricultural equipment.&rdquo;`} verdict={`Finance printed C$${financeCoveredCad} billion of imports from the U.S., about US$${computed.usdCoveredRounded} billion at the August 25 Bank of Canada rate. Steel, dairy, and agricultural equipment are on the Finance list. The fact sheet does not print the Canadian-dollar figure.`} chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="wh-fact-sheet-2026-09-08" passage="about $20 billion of U.S. exports" /><Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="products covering $27.6 billion in imports from the U.S." /></OutletCheck>
        <OutletCheckFold count={1} method="the Census export series and the Finance table.">
          <OutletCheck folded outlet="Associated Press" claim={`wrote that the Canadian tariffs &ldquo;cover about $20 billion in American goods, roughly 6% of the $333.6 billion the United States exported to Canada last year.&rdquo; PBS NewsHour carried the same dispatch.`} verdict={`The Census 2025 export total is ${census2025.exports.toLocaleString("en-US")} million dollars, US$${computed.censusExports2025Rounded} billion as rounded. Twenty billion is ${computed.share20OfUsExportsPct} percent of that total. The arithmetic checks out on the Census series.`} chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="ap-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="roughly 6% of the $333.6 billion" /></OutletCheck>
        </OutletCheckFold>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="claim-check">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Dollar for dollar, rate for rate</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Claim checked against the record</h2>

        <div data-layer="fact">
          <CheckedBlock label="Canada matched the U.S. action dollar for dollar" variant="contested">
            <p>Finance Canada assigns C${financeCoveredCad} billion of covered trade to each side. Covered trade is the value of listed imports, not duty liability, revenue collected, trade displaced, or economic harm. Those are five different quantities. Finance's figure is the first.<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="products covering $27.6 billion in imports from the U.S." /></p>
            <p>Finance has not published that valuation by tariff code and reference period. The figure did not change when {removedCodes} seafood lines left the list, and Finance has not said why. See the first catch row.<Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="List updated as of August 26, 2026" /></p>
            <p>Finance also says each Canadian rate is "based on the matching U.S. rate for the same goods." Finance's rate column matches the Canadian orders line for line, which shows the table matches Canadian law. It does not show that each Canadian rate matches the U.S. rate on the same goods, because no product-level match has been published.<Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="based on the matching U.S. rate for the same goods" /></p>
            <p>Champagne's quoted line is "dollar-for-dollar, rate for rate." Both halves are Finance's characterizations. The White House fact sheet does not print a matching dollar total. BBC converted the Canadian side as {bbcCovered}.<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="dollar-for-dollar, rate for rate" /></p>
          </CheckedBlock>
        </div>

        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>how the 6 percent and the conversions were counted</summary>
          <p>Spring Economic Update 2026 intro: approximately {springUpdateTariffFreePct} percent of goods trade tariff-free; chart 0.7 Average Tariff Rate Canada {springUpdateAvgTariffCanada} Global {springUpdateAvgTariffGlobal}. Gazette SOR/2025-181: ${rollbackConsumer2025} billion consumer goods and ${rollbackOther2025} billion other goods removed from September 1, 2025. Census c1220 TOTAL 2025 exports 333,619.7 million U.S. dollars. 20,000 / 333,619.7 = 0.05995, {computed.share20OfUsExportsPct} percent as rounded in the story. Census c1220 TOTAL 2025 imports 381,922.1 million. 20,000 / 381,922.1 = 0.05236, {computed.share20OfUsImportsPct} percent. Bank of Canada FXUSDCAD August 25 = {fxUsdCad20260825}. {financeCoveredCad} / {fxUsdCad20260825} = {computed.usdCovered}. {supportPackage} / {fxUsdCad20260825} = {computed.usdSupport}. The 17 days are 2026-09-08 minus 2026-08-22. The 33 days are 2026-08-22 minus 2026-07-20. The original Section 338 clock is 2026-08-19 minus 2026-07-20 = {daysJulyProcToOriginalEffective} days. The September additions are 2026-09-15 minus 2026-09-08 = {daysSeptModifyToEffective} days. PC 2026-0785 English Schedules 1 to 3: {oic0785Schedule1} + {oic0785Schedule2} + {oic0785Schedule3} = {oic0785Items}. PC 2026-0786 English Schedules 1, 1.1, 2, 2.1: {oic0786Schedule1} + {oic0786Schedule11} + {oic0786Schedule2} + {oic0786Schedule21} = {oic0786Items}. Overlap {oicOverlap}. Union {oic0785Items} + {oic0786Items} = {oicUnionItems}, matching the Finance August 26 HTML table of {listItems} unique codes. Finance last-column rates versus those schedules: {financeRateMismatches} mismatches. Support package 1.5 + 0.5 + 2 + 3.5 = {computed.supportSum}. August 25 unique codes {listItemsAug25} minus {removedCodes} chapter 03 plus {addedCodes} added = {listItems}. July 20 Annex I unique HTSUS 8-digit lines: {annexJulyDairyItems} + {annexJulyAlcoholItems} + {annexJulyMotorItems} = {annexJulyItems}. September 8 annex counts are HTSUS product-code lines, including {annexAlcoholScopeAddItems} added and {annexAlcoholScopeRemoveItems} removed on the alcohol-scope annex.</p>
        </details>
      </section>

      <section id="what-happened-next">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The same Tuesday in Washington</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 8 the White House went past duties. Three proclamations exclude listed Canadian dairy, motor-vehicle, and alcoholic-beverage products from importation from 12:01 a.m. eastern on September 29.<Cite s="proc-exclude-dairy-2026-09-08" passage="12:01 a.m. eastern time on September 29, 2026" /> Two more change which goods pay the 50 percent from September 15: {annexAlcoholScopeAddItems} lines join the alcoholic-beverages basket and {annexAlcoholScopeRemoveItems} leave it, and the motor-vehicles basket changes the same way.<Cite s="proc-modify-motor-2026-09-08" passage="12:01 a.m. eastern time on September 15, 2026" /><Cite s="proc-modify-alcohol-2026-09-08" passage="12:01 a.m. eastern time on September 15, 2026" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">That evening Trump went further, on Truth Social: "I am hereby directing the GSA, working with the USTR, to take all necessary steps to REMOVE Canadian-origin products from GSA's Multiple Award Schedules unless Canada restores full and fair reciprocity for American Farmers and Companies."<Cite s="trump-truth-social-gsa-2026-09-08" passage="I am hereby directing the GSA, working with the USTR, to take all necessary steps to REMOVE Canadian-origin products from GSA's Multiple Award Schedules" /> The schedules are the catalogues federal agencies buy from, and he put them at more than $50 billion a year. The White House fact sheet of September 8 repeats the direction and does not report a removal.<Cite s="wh-fact-sheet-2026-09-08" passage="remove $50 billion dollars" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">He also threatened to block Bombardier, the Canadian planemaker, from selling aircraft in the United States unless it built them there. Bombardier answered that it employs thousands of workers in the United States.<Cite s="aljazeera-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="block Canada-based aircraft manufacturer Bombardier" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Formal talks have not resumed. A spokesman for Dominic LeBlanc, the minister responsible for Canada-U.S. trade, said officials "remain in contact even though formal talks have not resumed."<Cite s="ap-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="remain in contact even though formal talks have not resumed" /> The next dates are September 15, when the U.S. baskets change, and September 29, when the bans begin.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">How each side valued its list. The White House fact sheet gives no dollar figure for the goods under the July 20 proclamations; the trade representative's statement gives the American figure, "nearly $20 billion in imports from Canada".<Cite s="ustr-section-338-2026-07-20" passage="nearly $20 billion in imports from Canada" /> Finance Canada's releases do not say how it reached C${financeCoveredCad} billion, by tariff code and reference period; the August 27 release from Sarnia repeats the figure without a method.<Cite s="finance-sarnia-2026-08-27" passage="products covering $27.6 billion in imports from the U.S." /> Finance's table does not match each Canadian rate to the U.S. rate on the same goods, which is what its claim that each rate is "based on the matching U.S. rate for the same goods" would need.<Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="based on the matching U.S. rate for the same goods" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">What the surtax will collect. C${financeCoveredCad} billion is the value of the listed goods, not money coming in. Goods in transit, goods for re-export, refunds for hospitals, factories, food processors, and farms, and the automaker relief all reduce it, and there is no figure yet for what has come in.<Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0785" passage="are subject to a surtax" /><Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="products covering $27.6 billion in imports from the U.S." /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The dairy-basket change. The proclamation banning Canadian dairy refers to a same-day proclamation changing the dairy basket.<Cite s="proc-exclude-dairy-2026-09-08" passage="Modifying the Scope of Products of Canada Subject to the Additional Duties Imposed To Offset Canadian Discrimination Against the Commerce of the United States With Respect to Dairy" /> As of September 9 the White House's list of presidential actions showed the alcohol-basket change and the dairy ban but no dairy-basket change, and the Federal Register's public-inspection desk listed no such document.<Cite s="wh-presidential-actions-listing-2026-09-09" passage="Excluding Certain Canadian Products from Importation into the United States in Response to Continued Discrimination Against the Commerce of the United States with Respect to Dairy" /><Cite s="fr-public-inspection-2026-09-09" passage="Federal Register Documents Currently on Public Inspection" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Whether the order took effect on September 8. The order comes into force on September 8, or on its registration day if that is later. The customs agency says September 8. The published order does not show a registration date.<Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0785" passage="if it is registered after that day, it comes into force on the day on which it is registered" /></p>

        <p data-layer="fact">No Canadian court filing or World Trade Organization complaint on the September 8 measures has been made public.</p>
      </section>

      <p class="railed rail-note" data-layer="fact">Story updated {event.updated}.</p>
